    "body": "The U.S. Department of Transportation announced $498 million for 35 port projects across 19 states, including dock replacements, cargo equipment, rail connections and terminal upgrades.\nWhat’s Related\nThe awards come through the Maritime Administration’s Port Infrastructure Development Program and cover coastal seaports, Great Lakes ports and inland river ports. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy said the investment aims to improve supply chains and lower shipping costs.\nThe announcement highlights seven projects, ranging from a new seafood pier in Maine to dock replacements in Alaska and infrastructure monitoring technology in Texas .\nRELATED: DOT Awards $1.73B in Infrastructure Grants. Here’s Where It’s Going\nWhere the money is going\nWhittier, Alaska: $50,593,488. The city will replace the DeLong Dock, add a seasonal floating dock and a dock manager building, and acquire three pedestal cranes and an integrated tug and barge.\nPort Everglades, Florida: $30,750,000. The Fort Lauderdale port will strengthen its liquid bulk terminal and widen the slip to accommodate larger ships. The work is intended to help maintain critical fuel operations during severe weather.\nRockport Harbor, Maine: $698,750. The harbor will build a 20-by-30-foot fixed timber pier to improve seafood transfers from vessels to trucks . Construction includes 12 timber piles, marine-grade connectors, and a vessel fendering system.\nRed Wing Port Authority, Minnesota: $10,161,985. The authority will rebuild its barge loading system, conveyors , electric winch, and dust collection systems. The project will increase cargo throughput and raise loading infrastructure above the Mississippi River floodplain.\nNorth Carolina State Ports Authority, Morehead City: $11,250,000. The project includes a 75-foot pile-supported concrete deck for heavy-lift cargo, a new crane rail and two on-dock rail extensions. Funding also covers security technology and optical character recognition software for tracking.\nPort Freeport, Texas: $9,412,500. The port will replace a 25-year-old bulk rice loader, install storage racks, and move the rice storage shed’s southern wall to expand berthing space.\nPort of Corpus Christi Authority, Texas: $7,153,376. The authority will acquire a digital twin, or virtual model, along with sensors and software to monitor pipeline, rail, road, and waterway infrastructure. Equipment includes laser scanning technology, sonar, high-definition cameras, and transponders.\nMARAD says the program aims to reduce shipping time and costs through port improvements. The release does not provide construction start dates or completion schedules for the highlighted projects.\nMore DOT funding for freight infrastructure\nThe latest port funding adds to DOT grants announced this year for rail improvements, truck parking, and other transportation projects.\nA separate $2.3 billion grant will fund 105 rail projects across 45 states and Washington, D.C. The awards include freight bridge repairs, track upgrades, and $30 million to rehabilitate and expand Texas City Terminal Railway’s rail yard and realign a port vehicle entrance.\nTruck parking is another focus , with $200 million awarded to 14 projects in 12 states. Projects include approximately 275 truck spaces near the Port of Savannah and about 140 truck spaces and 50 container staging positions at the Port of Wilmington.\nThrough its BUILD program , DOT also awarded $1.73 billion for 127 transportation projects. That funding included $136.8 million for port infrastructure and $87.7 million for freight rail, along with investments in roads, bridges, and truck parking.",
    "body_sha256": "51953008873a437c573c7bf724dd1f6fa8d6dfb9a352baaebcd895c966c3e9e9",
